<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-combined-and-joint-variation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Combined and Joint Variation</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.5 Formulae and Variations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Solve combined, joint and partial variations in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>15 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>To enable learners to discover the equation for combined variation by investigating the relationship between pressure, temperature, and volume.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Teacher's Role</title>
            <p>Facilitate group discussions as learners investigate the relationship between pressure, temperature, and volume. Encourage them to identify the constant of variation, write the variation equation, test it using new values, and explain how changing one variable affects the others.</p>
        </li>
    </dl>
</axiom>

<activity>
    <statement>
        <title>Activity: Investigating Pressure and Combined Variation</title>

        <p><em>Work in groups of 3–4.</em></p>

        <title>Real-World Scenario</title>

        <p>In physics, the pressure (<strong>P</strong>) of a gas varies directly with its temperature (<strong>T</strong>) and inversely with its volume (<strong>V</strong>).</p>

        <title>Group Investigation</title>

        <p>A gas has:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>Pressure = 10 units</li>
            <li>Temperature = 100 units</li>
            <li>Volume = 5 units</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Work together to answer the following:</p>

        <ol>
            <li>Calculate <strong>T ÷ V</strong>.</li>
            <li>How does this value compare with the pressure?</li>
            <li>Find the constant number that links <strong>T ÷ V</strong> to the pressure.</li>
            <li>Write an equation relating <strong>P</strong>, <strong>T</strong>, <strong>V</strong>, and your constant.</li>
        </ol>

        <title>Apply Your Rule</title>

        <p>Use your equation to find the pressure in each case.</p>

        <table>
            <tabular top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Trial</cell>
                    <cell>Temperature (T)</cell>
                    <cell>Volume (V)</cell>
                    <cell>Pressure (P)</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>A</cell>
                    <cell>200</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>?</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>B</cell>
                    <cell>100</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell>
                    <cell>?</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>C</cell>
                    <cell>300</cell>
                    <cell>15</cell>
                    <cell>?</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <title>Discuss</title>

        <ul>
            <li>What happens to the pressure when the temperature increases while the volume remains constant?</li>
            <li>What happens to the pressure when the volume increases while the temperature remains constant?</li>
            <li>How does this activity illustrate combined variation?</li>
        </ul>

    </statement>
</activity>

<axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Structured Instruction</title>
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>10 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>To formalize the concept of combined variation and introduce joint variation as another type of multi-variable variation.</p>
        </li>
    </dl>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p><strong>Combined Variation:</strong> A variable varies directly with one quantity and inversely with another. <m>y=\frac{kx}{z}</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><strong>Joint Variation:</strong> A variable varies directly with the product of two or more quantities. <m>y=kxz</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><strong>Constant of Variation:</strong> The value of <m>k</m> remains constant.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </paragraphs>
</axiom>

<insight>

    <p>Variation can involve more than one variable. Two common types are <strong>joint variation</strong> and <strong>combined variation</strong>.</p>

    <p><strong>1. Joint Variation</strong></p>

    <ul>
        <li>A variable varies directly with the product of two or more variables.</li>
        <li><strong>Equation:</strong> <m>y = kxz</m></li>
        <li><strong>Example:</strong> The area of a triangle varies jointly with its base and height: <m>A=\frac{1}{2}bh</m>.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure xml:id="fig-joint-variation-area">
        <caption>Joint variation showing area increasing as height increases for a fixed base.</caption>
        <image width="100%">
            <!-- Existing graph -->
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p><strong>2. Combined Variation</strong></p>

    <ul>
        <li>A variable varies directly with one variable and inversely with another.</li>
        <li><strong>Equation:</strong> <m>y=\frac{kx}{z}</m></li>
        <li><strong>Example:</strong> The pressure of a gas varies directly with temperature and inversely with volume: <m>P=\frac{kT}{V}</m>.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure xml:id="fig-combined-variation-example">
        <caption>Pressure decreases as volume increases when temperature is fixed.</caption>
        <image width="100%">
            <!-- Existing graph -->
        </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-combined-variation-workers">
        <caption>Time decreases as the number of workers increases for a fixed amount of work.</caption>
        <image width="100%">
            <!-- Existing graph -->
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p><strong>Summary</strong></p>

    <tabular top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell>Joint Variation</cell>
            <cell>Combined Variation</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>y = kxz</m></cell>
            <cell><m>y = \frac{kx}{z}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Direct with two or more variables.</cell>
            <cell>Direct with one variable and inverse with another.</cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>

</insigh>
    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The cost of painting a wall (<m>C</m>) varies jointly with its length (<m>l</m>) and its height (<m>h</m>). If a wall 10 meters long and 3 meters high costs Ksh300 to paint, how much will it cost to paint a wall 15 meters long and 4 meters high?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: Since it is joint variation, use <m>C = klh</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute the known values (<m>C=300, l=10, h=3</m>).</p>
            <p><m>300 = k(10)(3)</m></p>
            <p><m>300 = 30k \rightarrow k = 10</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>C = 10lh</m>.</p>
            <p>Solve for the new wall: Substitute the new values (<m>l=15, h=4</m>).</p>
            <p><m>C = 10(15)(4)</m></p>
            <p><m>C = 10(60) = 600</m></p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-joint-painting-cost-example">
                <caption>Joint variation graph for painting cost with fixed length, showing cost increasing directly with height.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=1,
                            ytick distance=150,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={Cost ($C$) Ksh},
                            xlabel={Height ($h$) m},
                            xmin=0, xmax=6, ymin=0, ymax=900,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=2, domain=0:5] {150*x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(1,150) (2,300) (3,450) (4,600)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The cost will be Ksh600.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The pressure (<m>P</m>) of a gas varies directly with its temperature (<m>T</m>) and inversely with its volume (<m>V</m>). A gas has a pressure of 150 units when the temperature is 300K and the volume is 20 liters. Find the pressure when the temperature is 400K and the volume is 10 liters.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: Combined variation uses <m>P = \frac{kT}{V}</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute <m>P=150, T=300, V=20</m>.</p>
            <p><m>150 = \frac{k(300)}{20}</m></p>
            <p><m>150 = 15k \rightarrow k = 10</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>P = \frac{10T}{V}</m>.</p>
            <p>Solve for the new pressure: Substitute <m>T=400, V=10</m>.</p>
            <p><m>P = \frac{10(400)}{10}</m></p>
            <p><m>P = 400</m></p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-combined-pressure-example">
                <caption>Combined variation graph for pressure at fixed temperature showing the inverse effect of volume.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=2,
                            ytick distance=50,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={Pressure ($P$)},
                            xlabel={Volume ($V$)},
                            xmin=0, xmax=22, ymin=0, ymax=220,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=200, domain=0.5:20] {400/x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(10,40) (20,20)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The new pressure is 400 units.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The weight (<m>W</m>) that a flatbed truck can carry varies jointly with the number of axles (<m>a</m>) and the tire pressure (<m>p</m>). A truck with 2 axles and 80 psi tire pressure can carry 16 tons. How many tons can a truck with 3 axles and 90 psi carry?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: <m>W = kap</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute <m>W=16, a=2, p=80</m>.</p>
            <p><m>16 = k(2)(80)</m></p>
            <p><m>16 = 160k \rightarrow k = 0.1</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>W = 0.1ap</m>.</p>
            <p>Solve for the new weight: Substitute <m>a=3, p=90</m>.</p>
            <p><m>W = 0.1(3)(90)</m></p>
            <p><m>W = 0.1(270) = 27</m></p>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The truck can carry 27 tons.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The frequency (<m>f</m>) of a vibrating string varies directly as the square root of the tension (<m>t</m>) and inversely as the length (<m>l</m>) of the string. If a string 2 meters long with a tension of 100N has a frequency of 40Hz, what is the frequency if the length is 1 meter and the tension is 400N?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: <m>f = \frac{k\sqrt{t}}{l}</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute <m>f=40, l=2, t=100</m>.</p>
            <p><m>40 = \frac{k\sqrt{100}}{2}</m></p>
            <p><m>40 = \frac{10k}{2} \rightarrow 40 = 5k \rightarrow k = 8</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>f = \frac{8\sqrt{t}}{l}</m>.</p>
            <p>Solve for the new frequency: Substitute <m>l=1, t=400</m>.</p>
            <p><m>f = \frac{8\sqrt{400}}{1}</m></p>
            <p><m>f = 8(20) = 160</m></p>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The new frequency is 160 Hz.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The simple interest (<m>I</m>) earned on a savings account varies jointly with the principal amount (<m>P</m>) and the time in years (<m>t</m>). If Ksh500 earns Ksh60 in 3 years, how much interest will Ksh800 earn in 5 years?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: <m>I = kPt</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute <m>I=60, P=500, t=3</m>.</p>
            <p><m>60 = k(500)(3)</m></p>
            <p><m>60 = 1500k \rightarrow k = \frac{60}{1500} = 0.04</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>I = 0.04Pt</m>. (Note: <m>k</m> here represents the annual interest rate of 4%).</p>
            <p>Solve for the new interest: Substitute <m>P=800, t=5</m>.</p>
            <p><m>I = 0.04(800)(5)</m></p>
            <p><m>I = 32(5) = 160</m></p>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The interest earned will be Ksh160.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

     <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
<exercises>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>The area of a triangle (A) varies jointly with its base (b) and height (h): <m>A = \frac{1}{2}bh</m>. Assume the base is fixed at 5 cm. Draw a graph of <m>A</m> against <m>h</m> for heights <m>h = 2, 4, 6, 8</m>. Label the axes and plot the points.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>With <m>b = 5</m>, the specific equation is <m>A = \frac{1}{2}(5)h = 2.5h</m>. The graph is a straight line through the origin, increasing with height.</p>
            <p>Points to plot: <m>(2, 5), (4, 10), (6, 15), (8, 20)</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-triangle-area-exercise">
                <caption>Graph of <m>A = 2.5h</m> showing triangle area varying jointly with height for a fixed base of 5 cm.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=1,
                            ytick distance=5,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={Area ($A$) cm$^2$},
                            xlabel={Height ($h$) cm},
                            xmin=0, xmax=9, ymin=0, ymax=25,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=2, domain=0:8] {2.5*x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(2,5) (4,10) (6,15) (8,20)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>The time to complete a job (T) varies directly with the area (A) and inversely with the number of workers (w): <m>T = \frac{kA}{w}</m>. If <m>T = 100</m> hours when <m>A = 400</m> and <m>w = 2</m>, draw a graph showing how <m>T</m> changes as <m>w</m> increases from 2 to 6. Label the axes and plot the values.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>First find <m>k</m>: <m>100 = \frac{k(400)}{2}</m>, so <m>k = 0.5</m>. The specific equation is <m>T = \frac{0.5(400)}{w} = \frac{200}{w}</m>.</p>
            <p>Plot values: <m>w=2 \rightarrow T=100</m>, <m>w=3 \rightarrow T \approx 66.7</m>, <m>w=4 \rightarrow T=50</m>, <m>w=5 \rightarrow T=40</m>, <m>w=6 \rightarrow T \approx 33.3</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-combined-time-workers-exercise">
                <caption>Graph of <m>T = \frac{200}{w}</m> showing how time decreases as the number of workers increases.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=1,
                            ytick distance=10,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={Time ($T$) hours},
                            xlabel={Workers ($w$)},
                            xmin=1.5, xmax=6.5, ymin=0, ymax=110,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=100, domain=2:6] {200/x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(2,100) (3,66.7) (4,50) (5,40) (6,33.3)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>The cost of painting a wall (C) varies jointly with the area of the wall (A) and the number of coats (n). If painting 20 m² with 2 coats costs Ksh 400, what is the cost of painting 30 m² with 3 coats?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: <m>C = kAn</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute <m>C=400, A=20, n=2</m>.</p>
            <p><m>400 = k(20)(2)</m></p>
            <p><m>400 = 40k \rightarrow k = 10</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>C = 10An</m>.</p>
            <p>Solve for the new cost: Substitute <m>A=30, n=3</m>.</p>
            <p><m>C = 10(30)(3)</m></p>
            <p><m>C = 10(90) = 900</m></p>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The cost will be Ksh 900.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>The volume of water (V) flowing through a pipe varies directly as the pressure (P) and inversely as the length of the pipe (L). If 10 liters flow through a 5 m pipe under 2 atm pressure, how much water flows through a 10 m pipe under 3 atm pressure?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: <m>V = \frac{kP}{L}</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute <m>V=10, P=2, L=5</m>.</p>
            <p><m>10 = \frac{k(2)}{5}</m></p>
            <p><m>10 = \frac{2k}{5} \rightarrow 50 = 2k \rightarrow k = 25</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>V = \frac{25P}{L}</m>.</p>
            <p>Solve for the new volume: Substitute <m>P=3, L=10</m>.</p>
            <p><m>V = \frac{25(3)}{10}</m></p>
            <p><m>V = \frac{75}{10} = 7.5</m></p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-combined-pipe-flow-exercise">
                <caption>Combined variation graph for pipe flow, showing how volume changes with pressure at fixed pipe length.</caption>
                <image width="100%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            grid=both,
                            minor grid style={draw=gray!15, line width=0.2pt},
                            major grid style={draw=gray!40, line width=0.4pt},
                            minor tick num=1,
                            xtick distance=1,
                            ytick distance=2,
                            axis lines=left,
                            ylabel={Volume ($V$) liters},
                            xlabel={Pressure ($P$) atm},
                            xmin=0, xmax=6, ymin=0, ymax=16,
                            width=6.5cm, height=7cm,
                            tick label style={font=\small},
                            label style={font=\small}
                        ]
                        \addplot[red, samples=100, domain=0:5.5] {2.5*x};
                        \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,0) (2,5) (3,7.5) (4,10)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 7.5 liters of water will flow.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>The brightness of a light (B) varies directly as the square of the voltage (V) and inversely as the distance from the source (d). If the brightness is 100 units at 10V and 2m distance, what is the brightness at 15V and 3m distance?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write the general formula: <m>B = \frac{kV^2}{d}</m>.</p>
            <p>Find the constant (<m>k</m>): Substitute <m>B=100, V=10, d=2</m>.</p>
            <p><m>100 = \frac{k(10)^2}{2}</m></p>
            <p><m>100 = \frac{100k}{2} \rightarrow 100 = 50k \rightarrow k = 2</m></p>
            <p>Write the specific equation: <m>B = \frac{2V^2}{d}</m>.</p>
            <p>Solve for the new brightness: Substitute <m>V=15, d=3</m>.</p>
            <p><m>B = \frac{2(15)^2}{3}</m></p>
            <p><m>B = \frac{2(225)}{3}</m></p>
            <p><m>B = \frac{450}{3} = 150</m></p>
            <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The brightness will be 150 units.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</exercises>

