    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Differentiation</title>

    <table>
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Provide guided examples that separately model inverse, joint, and combined variation before asking learners to compare them.</line>
                    <line>Use visual organizers and partially completed formula templates to help learners identify the correct variation relationship.</line>
                    <line>Encourage the use of tables and concrete representations to reveal constant products and direct relationships between variables.</line>
                </cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                </cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Challenge learners to derive variation equations from unfamiliar contexts and justify the structure of their models.</line>
                    <line>Ask learners to create their own real-life examples of joint and combined variation and exchange them with peers for solution.</line>
                    <line>Provide extension problems involving powers and roots, such as variables that vary directly as a square or inversely as a square root.</line>
                </cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>
</axiom>

